    {
      "name": "Antigrav Columns",
      "elements": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "text": "Antigrav Columns"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Antigrav columns run on HEAT, not FE. Heat comes in from below through a heat pipe from the heat network: a thermogenerator warms the pipe, and the pipe feeds heat into the column base. The column stores it in a buffer (up to 4000) and starts working for the magnet once warm (from 200). The hotter the column, the greater its contribution to stability."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each fully heated column raises the safe quench threshold by 7.5% over the base 50%. Up to 8 columns within 16 blocks of the magnet center are counted. Rough guide: 2 columns hold the threshold at 65%, 4 at 80% (needed for the superconductor coil), 6 at 95%, 7 the full 100% (needed for the graviton coil). The copper coil needs no columns: its ceiling sits below 50%."
        },
        {
          "type": "items",
          "items": [
            "industrial_core:antigrav_column",
            "industrial_core:heat_pipe",
            "industrial_core:thermogenerator"
          ],
          "label": "Heat feed"
        }
      ]
    }
